SIGNUP FLOW (1-Minute Explanation)

“In the signup flow, the client sends a POST /api/signup request with user details in JSON format.
The controller receives the request and converts the JSON payload
into a User entity using @RequestBody and delegates processing to the service layer.
In the service layer, the plain-text password is securely hashed using
BCryptPasswordEncoder, which is a one-way hashing algorithm with salting.
The hashed password and user data are then persisted using Spring Data JPA.
The repository internally uses Hibernate ORM to generate the SQL INSERT
statement and save the entity in the database.
Finally, the persisted entity with the generated primary key is returned to the client.”



1️⃣ High-Level Architecture

“My application follows a layered architecture with clear separation of concerns.”

Client → Controller → Service → Repository → Database

Controller layer handles HTTP requests and responses
Service layer contains business logic
Repository layer interacts with database using Spring Data JPA
Entity layer maps Java objects to database tables using ORM


2️⃣ Request Lifecycle – SIGNUP FLOW
Step 1: Client sends HTTP request
“The client sends a POST /api/signup request with JSON payload.”

Step 2: Controller layer

Technical explanation:
@RestController marks the class as REST endpoint provider
@PostMapping maps HTTP POST request
@RequestBody uses Jackson ObjectMapper to convert JSON into a User object
Controller delegates processing to Service layer (no business logic here)



Step 3: Service layer (Business Logic)

Technical explanation:

Service layer applies business rules
Password is hashed using BCryptPasswordEncoder
BCrypt is a one-way hashing algorithm with salt
Prevents rainbow-table attacks
Hashed password is stored instead of plain text
Service calls Repository to persist data

Step 4: Repository layer

Technical explanation:

Repository extends JpaRepository
Uses Spring Data JPA
Spring automatically generates implementation at runtime
Internally uses Hibernate ORM
Hibernate converts entity to SQL:

Database generates primary key (IDENTITY)
Hibernate returns managed entity with generated ID

Step 5: Response back to client

Service returns persisted entity
Controller returns it as JSON response
Jackson serializes Java object → JSON